<div class="lead-box">
    <p><strong>Software architecture</strong> is the big-picture design of an application. It explains how different parts of the system are organized, how they talk to each other, where data flows, and what rules keep the system reliable, secure, and easy to grow.</p>
    <p>Before writing many classes, controllers, or APIs, teams first need a clear structure. That structure is architecture. A good architecture saves time, reduces confusion, and helps a project remain stable even when users, features, and integrations increase.</p>
</div>

<div class="topic-meta">
    <span class="topic-chip">Foundation Topic</span>
    <span class="topic-chip">Microservices Path</span>
    <span class="topic-chip">System Design Basics</span>
    <span class="topic-chip">ASP.NET Core Ready</span>
</div>

<section class="section">
    <h3>1. Simple Definition</h3>
    <p>Think of software architecture like the plan of a large building. A building needs rooms, doors, wiring, water pipes, and safety rules. In the same way, a software system needs modules, APIs, databases, communication paths, security, and deployment rules.</p>

    <div class="note-box">
        <strong>Easy way to remember:</strong> code tells the computer <em>what to do</em>, while architecture decides <em>how the whole system is organized</em>.
    </div>

    <div class="picture-box">
        <div class="picture-head">Picture Idea: Software Architecture as a Building Blueprint</div>
        <div class="picture-body">
            <div class="building">
                <div class="building-block">User Interface</div>
                <div class="building-block">Business Logic</div>
                <div class="building-block">APIs / Services</div>
                <div class="building-block">Database</div>
                <div class="building-block">Security & Logs</div>
            </div>
            <div class="foundation">ARCHITECTURE = THE FOUNDATION + STRUCTURE + CONNECTION RULES</div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>2. Why Software Architecture Matters</h3>
    <div class="visual-grid">
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Better clarity</h4>
            <p>When architecture is planned properly, developers know where code should go, which layer handles which task, and how modules connect.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Easier maintenance</h4>
            <p>Well-structured systems are easier to debug, update, test, and extend because responsibilities are separated clearly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Scalability</h4>
            <p>Architecture helps a system grow when more users, more features, or more traffic arrive.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Team productivity</h4>
            <p>When structure is consistent, multiple developers can work on the same application without stepping on each other’s code.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>3. What Decisions Belong to Architecture?</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="topic-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Architecture Decision</th>
                    <th>What It Means</th>
                    <th>Example</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Application style</td>
                    <td>Choosing monolith, layered architecture, microservices, event-driven system, and so on.</td>
                    <td>An e-commerce portal may start as layered ASP.NET Core and later move to microservices.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Module boundaries</td>
                    <td>Deciding which part handles products, orders, payments, users, notifications, and reporting.</td>
                    <td>Order management should not contain payment gateway code directly.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Communication</td>
                    <td>Defining how parts exchange data.</td>
                    <td>One service may call another through REST API or messaging.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Data ownership</td>
                    <td>Deciding where data lives and who controls it.</td>
                    <td>Customer data may stay in Customer Service database only.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Security</td>
                    <td>Planning authentication, authorization, token flow, and data protection.</td>
                    <td>JWT tokens may be used to secure ASP.NET Core APIs.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Deployment</td>
                    <td>Defining how the app runs in development, testing, and production.</td>
                    <td>Containers, CI/CD, cloud hosting, load balancing.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>4. Diagram: Where Architecture Sits in a Project</h3>
    <p>Architecture stands above individual screens and functions. It controls the relationship between all major pieces.</p>
    <div class="diagram-box">Users
  │
  ▼
Frontend / UI Layer
  │
  ▼
API Layer / Controllers
  │
  ▼
Business Layer / Services
  │
  ├────────► External Services
  │              │
  │              └── Payment / Email / SMS / Maps
  │
  ▼
Data Access Layer / Repositories
  │
  ▼
Database / Storage

Architecture defines:
- where each responsibility should live
- how each layer communicates
- what rules the team must follow</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>5. Architecture vs Design vs Code</h3>
    <div class="compare-grid">
        <div class="compare-card">
            <h4>Architecture</h4>
            <p>Focuses on the big structure of the system.</p>
            <p><strong>Question answered:</strong> How will the whole application be organized?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card">
            <h4>Design</h4>
            <p>Focuses on detailed solutions inside modules or components.</p>
            <p><strong>Question answered:</strong> How will this feature or module work internally?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card">
            <h4>Code</h4>
            <p>Focuses on the actual implementation using classes, methods, APIs, DTOs, and logic.</p>
            <p><strong>Question answered:</strong> How do we build it line by line?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card">
            <h4>Simple example</h4>
            <p>Architecture says we need Product Service and Order Service. Design says how Order Service validates stock. Code implements the validation method.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>6. Common Architecture Styles</h3>
    <div class="mini-grid">
        <div class="mini-card">
            <h4>Monolithic</h4>
            <p>All modules are built and deployed as one application. Easier to start, harder to scale later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
            <h4>Layered Architecture</h4>
            <p>Application is divided into presentation, business, and data layers. Very common in ASP.NET projects.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="mini-card">
            <h4>Microservices</h4>
            <p>Application is split into small independent services, each handling a business capability.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-strip">
        <div class="flow-step">Small Application → Monolith</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Growing Application → Layered</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Large Distributed System → Microservices</div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>7. Real-World Example</h3>
    <p>Suppose a company is building an online shopping platform. The application contains products, customers, cart, orders, payments, shipping, reviews, and notifications.</p>

    <div class="note-box">
        Without architecture, developers may place all logic in random controllers, duplicate code, mix business rules with database code, and create a system that becomes difficult to test and extend.
    </div>

    <p>With proper architecture, the team decides:</p>
    <ul class="checklist">
        <li>where product logic should live</li>
        <li>how order processing should work</li>
        <li>how payment integration should be isolated</li>
        <li>how user authentication should protect the APIs</li>
        <li>how logs and monitoring should be captured</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>8. Why Software Architecture is Important for ASP.NET Core Microservices</h3>
    <p>Microservices are not just many APIs. They are a structured way of dividing a system into business-focused services. To do that correctly, teams need strong architecture decisions from the beginning.</p>

    <div class="path-grid">
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Clear service boundaries</h4>
            <p>Architecture decides what belongs in Product Service, Order Service, Payment Service, and Identity Service.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Independent deployment</h4>
            <p>Services can be built and released separately when their responsibilities are defined clearly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Better scaling strategy</h4>
            <p>If order traffic is high, Order Service can scale without forcing the whole system to scale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
            <h4>Safer long-term growth</h4>
            <p>Architecture keeps the system maintainable as more teams, features, integrations, and users are added.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>9. Picture-Like Diagram: From Business Need to Architecture</h3>
    <div class="diagram-box">Business Goal
  │
  ├── Sell products online
  ├── Accept payments
  ├── Track orders
  └── Notify customers
        │
        ▼
Architecture Planning
  │
  ├── Product Module / Product Service
  ├── Order Module / Order Service
  ├── Payment Module / Payment Service
  ├── Notification Module / Notification Service
  └── Security / Identity Layer
        │
        ▼
Implementation in ASP.NET Core
  │
  ├── Controllers
  ├── Services
  ├── Repositories
  ├── DTOs
  ├── JWT Authentication
  └── Database + Deployment</div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>10. Signs of Good Software Architecture</h3>
    <ul class="checklist">
        <li>The system is easy to understand.</li>
        <li>Each part has a clear responsibility.</li>
        <li>Changes in one area do not break many other areas.</li>
        <li>The application can be tested in an organized way.</li>
        <li>Security and logging are considered early, not added at the end.</li>
        <li>The system can grow without becoming messy.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>11. Signs of Poor Software Architecture</h3>
    <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Controllers become too large and contain everything.</li>
        <li>Business logic is duplicated across many places.</li>
        <li>Database code is mixed directly with UI logic.</li>
        <li>Small changes create errors in unrelated features.</li>
        <li>Testing becomes difficult because code is tightly coupled.</li>
        <li>New developers need too much time to understand the system.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>12. Daily-Life Analogy</h3>
    <p>Imagine a hospital. There is reception, billing, lab, pharmacy, doctor consultation, and emergency services. Each area has a separate role but all are connected. If everything is done at one desk, the hospital becomes slow and confusing.</p>
    <p>Software architecture works the same way. It divides work into organized units and defines how those units cooperate.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>13. Short Question and Answer</h3>
    <div class="qa-box">
        <div class="qa-item">
            <div class="qa-q">What is software architecture in one line?</div>
            <div class="qa-a">It is the high-level structure of a software system and the rules that guide how its parts work together.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-item">
            <div class="qa-q">Why should a project think about architecture early?</div>
            <div class="qa-a">Because early structure decisions affect maintainability, performance, scalability, testing, and future growth.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-item">
            <div class="qa-q">Is architecture only for big projects?</div>
            <div class="qa-a">No. Even small projects benefit from a clean structure. The level of complexity changes, but architecture always matters.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-item">
            <div class="qa-q">How is architecture related to microservices?</div>
            <div class="qa-a">Microservices are an architectural style where the system is split into independent services based on business capabilities.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h3>14. Final Summary</h3>
    <div class="note-box">
        <p>Software architecture is the starting blueprint of a successful application. It gives the system shape, direction, and discipline. It decides how modules are separated, how they communicate, how data is controlled, and how the application can grow safely over time.</p>
        <p>When learning ASP.NET Core microservices, understanding software architecture first is extremely important. Once the big picture is clear, topics such as layered architecture, APIs, service boundaries, API Gateway, authentication, Docker, and Kubernetes become much easier to understand.</p>
    </div>
</section>
